List an archive's entries. Without `flat`, one folder level at a time (the root, or `dir`),
with folders that only exist as parts of paths shown too; with `flat` or a `glob`, every
entry under `dir`. Results come in pages with `offset` and `limit`.

Usage: beezip list [OPTIONS] <ARCHIVE> [DIR]

Arguments:
  <ARCHIVE>
          The archive

  [DIR]
          A folder inside the archive to list instead of the root

Options:
      --flat[=<FLAT>]
          Every entry below `dir`, not just one level
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --glob <PATTERN>
          Only entries matching these patterns (implies `flat`). Without `/` a pattern matches a
          name at any depth (`*.txt`); with `/` the whole path (`docs/**/*.md`)

      --offset <OFFSET>
          Entries to skip, for paging

      --limit <LIMIT>
          At most this many entries (over MCP, at most 500)

      --sort <SORT>
          Sort by this, folders first [default: archive order]

          Possible values:
          - name
          - size
          - packed
          - modified
          - kind:      Folders, files, then links; by name within each
          - method:    The compression method's name; entries without one first
          - crc
          - encrypted: Unencrypted entries first

      --reverse[=<REVERSE>]
          Reverse the sort (folders stay first)
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --kind <KIND>
          Only entries of this kind: `file` (files and hard links), `dir`, or `link` (symlinks)

          Possible values:
          - file: Files and hard links (which extract as files)
          - dir
          - link: Symlinks

  -p, --password <PASSWORD>
          The password, if entry names need one

      --name-encoding <ENCODING>
          How names stored as bytes are read: zip names without the UTF-8 flag, tar names outside
          pax records [default: from config, `auto`]
          
          [possible values: auto, utf-8, cp437, cp850, cp852, cp866, cp874, cp932, cp936, cp949,
          cp950, cp1250, cp1251, cp1252, cp1253, cp1254, cp1255, cp1256, cp1257, cp1258, koi8-r,
          koi8-u, iso-8859-1, iso-8859-2, iso-8859-5, iso-8859-7, euc-jp, gb18030]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
